Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 10, verse 30:

His saying, exalted is He: ﴿There every soul will [come to] know what it sent forth﴾ – the verse.

In this noble verse he stated explicitly that on the Day of Resurrection every soul will "tabluu" (come to know, be informed of, and realize) what it sent forth, i.e., what it had put forward of good and evil. He explained this meaning in many verses, such as:

• His saying: ﴿On that Day man will be informed of what he sent forth and left behind﴾ [al-Qiyamah: 13];

• His saying: ﴿The Day when secrets will be laid bare﴾ [al-Tariq: 9];

• His saying: ﴿And We will bring out for him on the Day of Resurrection a record which he will find spread open. [It will be said:] "Read your record. Sufficient is yourself against you this Day as an accountant."﴾ [al-Isra': 13–14];

• His saying: ﴿They will say, "Woe to us! What is this record that leaves nothing small or great but has enumerated it?" And they will find what they did present there﴾ – the verse [al-Kahf: 49].

As for the reading "tatlu" with two taa's, there are two interpretations of the meaning of the verse:

The first: that "tatlu" means "taqra'" (reads), i.e., each soul reads in its record of deeds all that it sent forth, and this returns to the first reading.

The second: that every nation follows what it worshipped, in accordance with his saying ﷺ: "Every nation will follow whatever it used to worship, so whoever used to worship the sun will follow the sun," and the hadith continues.
